Introduction                                                                                                           

This document collects the scientific literature regarding brain.                                                      


Brain-Computer Interfaces and Neural Signal Processing                                                                 

Recent advances in EEG and MEG signal processing have significantly improved the reliability and accessibility of      
Brain-Computer Interfaces (BCIs). Machine learning approaches, particularly Random Forest algorithms, have demonstrated
robust performance in classifying EEG signals from real and imagery motor activities, even when using consumer-grade   
hardware[^2609.11695]. To address reproducibility in analyzing neuronal avalanches, the open-source package            
pyAvalanches provides a standardized pipeline for characterizing spatiotemporal propagation patterns in EEG            
recordings[^2609.11530]. In P300 speller systems, component anti-synergy has been identified, revealing that adding    
individually promising components like language models or spatial filters can sometimes degrade performance depending  
on the underlying EEG pipeline quality[^2609.10961]. Federated learning frameworks like NEXUS-MI further enhance       
MI-BCIs by coordinating personalization across gateways while reducing communication overhead[^2609.09786]. For        
clinical applications, EEGBind leverages EEG-centric multimodal binding to accurately classify source-level interictal 
epileptiform discharges, improving presurgical evaluation[^2609.09728]. Beyond clinical settings, brain-based digital  
twins are being developed to fuse consumer-grade EEG with kinematic data for real-time motor imagery feedback in sports
training[^10.17870_bathspa.00017805]. Additionally, research into infant development links EEG microstates to          
temperament attributes, offering new insights into early self-regulation and emotional reactivity[^10.7273_000007929]. 


Neuroimaging, Registration, and Spatial Analysis                                                                       

High-resolution neuroimaging and precise spatial registration remain critical for understanding brain structure and    
function. Cross-modal diffusion transformers, such as CoMA-DiT, are being utilized to augment multimodal brain state   
decoding by treating paired modalities as mutual generative supervision sources, yielding significant accuracy gains in
auditory attention and emotion recognition tasks[^2609.11341]. For MEG data, a novel regression-based framework        
utilizing Wendland radial basis functions on the cortical surface provides spatially resolved effect estimates,        
overcoming the limitations of traditional cluster-based permutation tests[^2609.11278]. In model systems, deep learning
pipelines now enable rapid, high-resolution deformable registration of Drosophila larval brains, outperforming         
classical methods in both speed and accuracy[^2609.11240]. Human brain MRI registration has also advanced with         
two-parameter flow map learning, which directly learns continuous-time non-autonomous ODE solutions to preserve        
diffeomorphic structure while improving alignment accuracy[^2609.10789]. Furthermore, LUCID integrates multi-view      
diffusion priors with projection-domain data consistency to resolve missing-cone artifacts in laminographic X-ray      
nanoimaging of brain tissue, enhancing spatial fidelity for connectomics[^2609.10456]. At the sensory level, spatial   
transcriptional codes have been shown to govern olfactory receptor choice, precisely aligning sensory maps between the 
nose and the brain[^10.5281_zenodo.18941307].                                                                          


Brain Aging, Neurodegeneration, and Biomarkers                                                                         

Computational phenotyping of brain aging is accelerating through longitudinal modeling and foundation models.          
Brain-PACE extends Siamese longitudinal frameworks to directly estimate structural brain aging pace from paired MRI,   
revealing stronger associations with tau burden and cognitive impairment than indirect cross-sectional                 
methods[^2609.11378]. The BrainTaskonomy framework organizes fMRI foundation model pretraining and adaptation using    
measured learning relations and priority-guided curricula, significantly reducing prediction errors across diverse     
tasks[^2609.10518]. Theoretical advances in covariance neural networks (VNNs) provide refined stability bounds and     
transferability insights for characterizing brain age gaps in neurodegenerative conditions[^2609.10490]. Explainable AI
(XAI) is also being integrated into brain-age prediction via automated explanation-knowledge loops that align model    
explanations with verified neurobiological literature[^2609.09388]. On a structural level, category fluency performance
is influenced by gray matter volumes in the DLPFC and IFG, with cultural and age-related variations highlighting the   
interplay between neural efficiency and cognitive strategies[^10.48617_etd.1671]. At the molecular level, circular RNAs
(circRNAs) accumulate in the Drosophila brain in correlation with biological age, serving as stable biomarkers for     
aging and insulin-related pathways[^10.48617_etd.1568]. Comprehensive arteriovenous metabolic profiling across human   
organs further quantifies brain-specific metabolite exchange, offering a quantitative atlas for identifying            
pathological features[^10.5281_zenodo.20652506].                                                                       


Brain Tumors, Metastases, and Therapeutics                                                                             

The diagnosis and treatment of brain tumors and metastases are being transformed by deep learning and targeted delivery
systems. For brain metastases, nnU-Net ensembles combined with rule-based post-processing achieve high lesion-wise Dice
similarity coefficients, though rigorous out-of-fold audits reveal that some leaderboard gains do not                  
generalize[^2609.11477]. Scale-aware 3D deep learning frameworks that fuse probability maps from different spatial     
fields of view improve lesion-level precision and reduce false positives in multimodal MRI detection[^2609.10825].     
Single-cell spatially resolved atlases using 10x Visium are mapping pan-cancer metastatic brain tumors, providing      
unprecedented cellular and spatial resolution of the tumor microenvironment[^10.5281_zenodo.21816284]. In Alzheimer's  
research, the amyloid precursor protein (APP) is increasingly recognized as a universal survival molecule, raising     
critical concerns about broad-spectrum APP suppression therapies that may disarm the brain's natural repair            
mechanisms[^10.5281_zenodo.20049820]. Therapeutic delivery across the blood-brain barrier is being advanced through    
lipid nanoparticle (LNP) formulations encapsulating siRNA targeting TERT in glioblastoma, demonstrating robust gene    
silencing and cytotoxic effects[^10.17030_uclan.thesis.00059253]. Additional LNP platforms loaded with epigallocatechin
gallate (EGCG) are being optimized to bypass the BBB and target glioma stem cells[^10.17030_uclan.thesis.00059033].    
Furthermore, secretomes and extracellular vesicles (EVs) derived from neural crest stem cells inhibit glioblastoma     
proliferation and normalize the anti-tumor immune environment by modulating NF-κB signaling[^10.48683_1926.00128572].  
Finally, RET signaling pathways are being investigated as key mediators in estrogen receptor-positive breast cancer    
brain metastasis[^10.5281_zenodo.19501083].                                                                            


Network Neuroscience and Dynamics                                                                                      

Understanding large-scale brain networks and neural dynamics continues to bridge computational modeling with biological
observation. The "Platonic brain bridge" hypothesis proposes that human brain networks can serve as architectural      
priors for omni-modal models, with cortical networks acting as experts that significantly boost model                  
accuracy[^2609.10947]. Cortical information transfer analysis using Permutation Transfer Entropy on EEG data           
demonstrates that hemispherical network dynamics during motor execution are conserved across right- and left-handed    
individuals, refuting assumptions of mirrored lateralization[^2609.10870]. Scalable neuronal reconstruction from       
petabyte-scale microscopy data is achieved through SkNeXt, a topology-first framework that uses compact SWC skeletons  
to reduce computational overhead and preserve long-range connectivity in mouse brain datasets[^2609.09832]. Theoretical
models of real neurons indicate that criticality emerges naturally as system size increases, resolving tensions between
fine-tuning requirements and observed neural activity patterns[^2609.09438]. At the brainstem level, cholecystokinin   
(CCK) signaling in the nucleus of the solitary tract (NTS) is blunted by high-fat diets, linking central vagal afferent
pathways to satiety regulation and obesity-related metabolic disruption[^10.7273_000008411].                           

